## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical knowledge of testing streaming systems, with emphasis on edge-case window behavior, fault tolerance, and end-to-end accuracy—skills essential for building production-grade data platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Windowing semantics and latency boundaries
- Idempotent and deduplicated alerting
- End-to-end streaming integration patterns
- Backpressure handling and burst tolerance
- Observability and feature drift detection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Unit test for window aggregation correctness
async function testWindowAggregation() {
  const windowService = new WindowingService({
    windowSizeMs: 5000,
    slideIntervalMs: 1000
  });

  // Simulate late-arriving events within watermark tolerance
  await windowService.processEvents([
    { timestamp: 1000, value: 10 },
    { timestamp: 2000, value: 20 },
    { timestamp: 5500, value: 30 } // Late event within tolerance
  ]);

  const result = await windowService.getWindowResult();
  expect(result.sum).toBe(60);
  expect(result.count).toBe(3);
}

// Integration test for alert deduplication
async function testAlertDeduplication() {
  const alertService = new AlertService();

  // Simulate duplicate events from Kafka retry
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    await alertService.process({
      eventId: 'evt-123',
      anomalyScore: 0.95,
      timestamp: Date.now()
    });
  }

  const alerts = await alertService.getEmittedAlerts();
  expect(alerts.length).toBe(1); // Only one alert emitted
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests resilience and reliability fundamentals using real-world API interaction patterns. It evaluates the ability to design concrete tests, leverage mocks effectively, and reason about backoff strategies, idempotency, and rate limits—core skills for building dependable services.

## Key Concepts

- API mocking with deterministic fixtures
- Retry and backoff strategies with configurable caps
- Idempotent retry semantics to ensure safety
- Per-client rate limiting under concurrent scenarios
- CI integration for reproducible, automated testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example scaffold using a mocking library
const nock = require('nock');
const { pricingClient } = require('./pricingClient');

describe('Pricing API Client', () => {
  beforeEach(() => nock.cleanAll());

  test('retries on 429 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(429, 'Too Many Requests', { 'Retry-After': '1' })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 99.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(99.99);
    expect(scope.isDone()).toBe(true);
  });

  test('retries on 500 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(500)
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 49.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(49.99);
  });

  test('respects max retry cap', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .times(4)
      .reply(500);

    await expect(pricingClient.getPrice()).rejects.toThrow('Max retries exceeded');
  });

  test('idempotent retries do not cause side effects', async () => {
    let callCount = 0;
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(() => {
        callCount++;
        return [500, {}];
      })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 100 });

    await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(callCount).toBe(1); // First call failed, second succeeded
  });
});
```

